---
<DocsLayout title="Data types" description="Reference for thinDB SQL data types, storage, aliases, and behavior." headings={headings}>
  <PageHeader eyebrow="Reference" title="Data types" description="thinDB uses a compact, strict type system designed for columnar analytics. SQL aliases improve client compatibility without changing the underlying representation." />
  <h2 id="type-table">Type table</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>SQL type</th><th>Aliases</th><th>Physical representation</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td><code>TINYINT</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Signed 8-bit integer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>SMALLINT</code></td><td><code>INT2</code></td><td>Signed 16-bit integer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>INT</code></td><td><code>INTEGER</code>, <code>INT4</code></td><td>Signed 32-bit integer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>BIGINT</code></td><td><code>INT8</code></td><td>Signed 64-bit integer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>LARGEINT</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Signed 128-bit integer.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>FLOAT</code></td><td><code>REAL</code>, <code>FLOAT4</code></td><td>IEEE 754 32-bit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DOUBLE</code></td><td><code>DOUBLE PRECISION</code>, <code>FLOAT8</code></td><td>IEEE 754 64-bit.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DECIMAL(p,s)</code></td><td><code>NUMERIC(p,s)</code>, <code>DECIMAL32/64/128(p,s)</code>, <code>DECIMAL256(p,s)</code></td><td>Scaled i64 for precision up to 18, i128 through 38, and i256 through 76.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>VARCHAR(n)</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Variable UTF-8 bytes; the declared bound is metadata.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>CHAR(n)</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Variable UTF-8 bytes without blank-padding; the declared bound is metadata.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>STRING</code></td><td><code>TEXT</code></td><td>Unbounded variable UTF-8 bytes.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>BOOLEAN</code></td><td><code>BOOL</code></td><td>One-byte value in fixed-width storage.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DATE</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>i32 days since 1970-01-01 UTC.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>DATETIME</code></td><td><code>TIMESTAMP</code>, <code>TIMESTAMPTZ</code></td><td>i64 microseconds since Unix epoch, timezone-naive.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>UUID</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Unsigned 128-bit value.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>JSON</code></td><td><code>JSONB</code></td><td>Validated JSON document with its own logical type.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>ARRAY&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>One self-contained variable-length cell per row.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>MAP&lt;K, V&gt;</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Keys and values as two arrays of one length in one cell.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>STRUCT&lt;name T, ...&gt;</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Fields in one cell; field names live in the type.</td></tr>
    <tr><td><code>BITMAP</code></td><td>&mdash;</td><td>A set of unsigned 64-bit integers, stored in StarRocks' serialized Roaring form.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>

  <h2 id="integers">Integer arithmetic</h2>
  <p>Integer arithmetic follows StarRocks. <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, and <code>*</code> widen the common type one level (<code>TINYINT</code> to <code>SMALLINT</code>, <code>SMALLINT</code> to <code>INT</code>, <code>INT</code> to <code>BIGINT</code>), and <code>BIGINT</code> results wrap in two's complement. <code>SUM</code> over integers up to <code>BIGINT</code> returns <code>BIGINT</code>, also wrapping; <code>SUM(LARGEINT)</code> returns <code>LARGEINT</code> and raises <code>ArithmeticOverflow</code> past the 128-bit range. A <code>CAST</code> of a number outside an integer type's range is NULL.</p>

  <h2 id="decimal">Decimal behavior</h2>
  <p>Precision is 1 through 76 and scale is 0 through precision. Overflow is an error rather than silent truncation. <code>SUM</code> of a decimal with at most 38 digits returns <code>DECIMAL(38, s)</code>, and of a wider one <code>DECIMAL(76, s)</code>; <code>AVG</code> returns <code>DOUBLE</code>. Arithmetic over operands of at most 38 digits keeps results within 38 digits. A <code>DECIMAL256</code> column has no min/max statistics, so only its key Bloom filter prunes on it.</p>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`CREATE TABLE ledger (
  entry_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  amount DECIMAL(18, 2) NOT NULL,
  rate DECIMAL(10, 6) NOT NULL
);

SELECT SUM(amount), ROUND(amount * rate, 2) FROM ledger GROUP BY entry_id;`} />

  <h2 id="temporal">Temporal behavior</h2>
  <p><code>DATE</code> and <code>DATETIME</code> support typed literals, extraction, interval arithmetic, formatting, grouping, ordering, predicates, and wire conversion. Month/year addition clamps to the last valid day of a shorter target month.</p>
  <Callout type="warning" title="No timezone identity"><p><code>TIMESTAMPTZ</code> is accepted as a compatibility alias for <code>DATETIME</code>. It does not retain an offset or timezone. Normalize timestamps at application boundaries; <code>convert_tz</code> and <code>from_unixtime(n, format, zone)</code> convert between zones explicitly.</p></Callout>

  <h2 id="strings-json-uuid">Strings, JSON, and UUID</h2>
  <p><code>VARCHAR(n)</code> and <code>CHAR(n)</code> bounds are metadata: longer values are stored whole, and <code>CHAR</code> is not blank-padded. JSON carries a distinct logical type for functions and operators. UUID uses numeric 128-bit ordering and canonical wire text.</p>

  <h2 id="nested">ARRAY, MAP, and STRUCT</h2>
  <CodeBlock language="sql" code={`CREATE TABLE sessions (
  session_id BIGINT PRIMARY KEY,
  pages ARRAY<VARCHAR(64)>,
  counters MAP<VARCHAR(16), INT>,
  device STRUCT<os VARCHAR(16), version INT>
);

INSERT INTO sessions VALUES
  (1, ['/home', '/pricing'], map{'clicks': 3}, named_struct('os', 'ios', 'version', 17));

SELECT pages[1], counters['clicks'], device.os, cardinality(pages)
FROM sessions;`} />
  <ul>
    <li>Literals: <code>[1, 2, 3]</code>, <code>map&#123;'k': v&#125;</code> or <code>map('k', v)</code>, and <code>row(...)</code>, <code>struct(...)</code>, or <code>named_struct('name', value, ...)</code>.</li>
    <li>Access: <code>arr[i]</code> counts from 1, <code>m[key]</code> reads a map, and <code>s.field</code> or <code>s[i]</code> reads a struct. A missing element or key is NULL.</li>
    <li>Values nest to any depth, compare and sort element-wise, and work as <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code>, and join keys. Columns of different nested types compare at their common type.</li>
    <li>Nested columns get no zone-map or Bloom pruning. The MySQL wire prints them as StarRocks does (<code>[1,2]</code>, <code>&#123;"a":1&#125;</code>); the PostgreSQL wire sends text.</li>
    <li>Array and map functions and lambdas are listed in <a href={withBase('/sql/functions/#nested')}>Built-in functions</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="bitmap">BITMAP</h2>
  <p>A <code>BITMAP</code> column holds a set of unsigned 64-bit integers for exact distinct counting and set algebra, built with <code>to_bitmap</code> or <code>bitmap_agg</code> and read with functions such as <code>bitmap_count</code> and <code>bitmap_union_count</code>. It has no literal and no default, does not nest inside other types, compares with nothing, and is sent over the wires as NULL, as StarRocks shows it. Use <code>bitmap_to_string</code> or <code>bitmap_to_array</code> to read its members.</p>

  <h2 id="nullability">Nullability and defaults</h2>
  <p>SQL DDL follows the standard nullable-by-default rule. <code>PRIMARY KEY</code>, <code>NOT NULL</code>, <code>SERIAL</code>, and <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> produce non-nullable columns. The embedded Zig schema API defaults to non-nullable and requires <code>.nullable = true</code> explicitly. The engine stores one validity bit per row only for nullable columns. A default is a literal, <code>NULL</code>, or <code>CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code>, and a <code>DATETIME</code> column may add <code>ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP</code>. <code>AUTO_INCREMENT</code> applies to one integer-family column per table.</p>
  <PageLinks previous={{ label: 'Storage and durability', href: '/operations/storage/' }} next={{ label: 'Compatibility', href: '/reference/compatibility/' }} />
</DocsLayout>
